{
 "Source Catalog": "INTERACT",
 "Date Metadata Harvested": "2026-04-21T14:26:53+00:00",
 "POSDT ID": "8595ce81-8e2d-dca3-2da2-60227a35e4c6",
 "Source Catalog URL": "https://interact-gis.org/Home/Stations",
 "Source Catalog Logo URL": "https://eu-interact.org/app/uploads/2024/11/logo_INPA_BLU_transparent.png",
 "Site Type": [
  "Research Station"
 ],
 "Site Name": "Oulanka Research Station",
 "Web Links": [
  "www.oulu.fi/oulankaresearchstation",
  "https://interact-gis.org/Home/Station/24"
 ],
 "Site IDs": [
  "Oulanka",
  24
 ],
 "Site Description": "<p>Oulanka Research Station is situated in the river valley of Oulanka within the Oulanka National Park (66°22’ N, 29°19’ E, 167 m a.s.l.). The station is located c. 25 km south of the Arctic Circle, 13 km west of the Russian border, about 280 km northeast of Oulu, and 55 km north of Kuusamo.</p><p>Oulanka National Park is a northern biodiversity hot-spot, thanks to the calcium-rich bedrock, varying topography, and large environmental gradients within a small area. Moreover, it has historically been at the cross-roads for movement of northern, southern and eastern species. Unlike most of Finland, the local rivers drain towards the northeast, i.e. to the White Sea.</p><p>The study area includes the towns of Kuusamo, Posio, Taivalkoski, and Salla and covers an area of roughly 100 &amp;times; 200 km. Most of the research activity is concentrated in and around the Oulanka National Park. Historically research has been focused on animal and plant ecology as well as geography. Recently sociological aspects have been included as well. Oulanka has a broad range of long-term data series available for researchers. These include both biological and physical/chemical datasets, and many of them have been collected since 1966. The datasets cover time-series on meteorology, water chemistry of streams and lakes, phenology, ice cover, snow cover, active layer depth, air pollution, as well as different plant and animal species, etc. Oulanka also offers a recently acquired, extremely comprehensive (70 &amp;times; 90 km) set of high-resolution aerial photographs covering three different areas, i.e. the early 1950&amp;rsquo;s, the early 1980&amp;rsquo;s, and c. 2005.</p>",
 "Metadata Updated": "2025-08-27T12:10:03+00:00",
 "Contact Name": [
  "Riku Paavola"
 ],
 "Organization": [
  "University of Oulu",
  "Thule institute"
 ],
 "Country": [
  "Finland"
 ],
 "Related Locations": [
  "Kuusamo (16 300 inhabitants)"
 ],
 "Elevation": 165,
 "Coordinates": "POINT (29.31199444 66.37001389)",
 "Networks": [
  "Interact"
 ],
 "Operating Status": "Open",
 "Start Year": 1966
}
{
 "Source Catalog": "INTERACT",
 "Date Metadata Harvested": "2026-04-21T14:26:53+00:00",
 "POSDT ID": "1fc379da-6cab-871b-0028-43d54f2e60f2",
 "Source Catalog URL": "https://interact-gis.org/Home/Stations",
 "Source Catalog Logo URL": "https://eu-interact.org/app/uploads/2024/11/logo_INPA_BLU_transparent.png",
 "Site Type": [
  "Research Station"
 ],
 "Site Name": "Pallas-Sodankylä Research Station",
 "Web Links": [
  "http://fmiarc.fmi.fi",
  "https://interact-gis.org/Home/Station/22"
 ],
 "Site IDs": [
  "PallSod",
  22
 ],
 "Site Description": "<p>Pallas is located in western Lapland (67°58’ N, 24°07’ E) in Pallas fell. The station is part of a national park with limited access to the public. \r\n\r\nThe Sodankylä facility (67°22’ N, 26°39’ E) is located in central Lapland within the boreal forest region. The station is not part of any national park, but the area (c. 2 km2) is dedicated to atmospheric and geological research, and therefore has limited public access. The area is surrounded by forest and wetland owned by the Finnish government.</p><p>The Finnish Meteorological Institute has a long history of atmospheric monitoring at Pallas; the first weather station was established near Lake Pallasjärvi in 1935. The measurements of atmospheric composition were started in 1991, and the Sammaltunturi station was established as a node of the Pallas–Sodankylä Global Atmosphere Watch (GAW) station in 1994. \r\n\r\nThe Sodankylä Station was established in 1949, but continuous homogenized synoptic weather records have been made since 1908. The facility consists currently of multiple buildings (1500 m2 in total).</p><p>The Pallas station consists of research infrastructure for monitoring and studying the atmosphere, ecosystems and their interactions. The research area hosts four sub-sites with different research programs within a 5 km radius, covering different micro-environments: top of an arctic fell, an elevated forest clearing, a forest site and a subarctic fen. \r\n\r\nThe Sodankylä facility hosts programs exploring upper-air chemistry and physics, atmospheric column measurements, snow/soil physics, biosphere-atmosphere interaction, and satellite calibration-validation studies.</p>",
 "Metadata Updated": "2025-08-27T12:08:46+00:00",
 "Contact Name": [
  "Antti Hyvärinen (Pallas) and Leena Leppänen (Sodankylä)"
 ],
 "Organization": [
  "Finnish Meteorological Institute"
 ],
 "Country": [
  "Finland"
 ],
 "Related Locations": [
  "Sodankylä (6000 inhabitants)"
 ],
 "Elevation": 179,
 "Coordinates": "POINT (24.11569714 67.97328022)",
 "Networks": [
  "Interact",
  "Polarin"
 ],
 "Operating Status": "Open",
 "Start Year": 1949
}
